{
    "background_story": "In ancient Mesopotamian mythology, Novah was revered as the goddess of fertility and childbirth, often invoked as Damkina. As the consort of Enki, the god of wisdom, Novah embodied the life-giving forces of the earth. Her domain was the sacred marriage rite, where she ensured the cycles of nature were maintained and the land remained fertile. Novah's power was said to reside in the primordial waters, where she nurtured the seeds of life. As Damkina, she protected the unborn and guided them into the world, watching over the delicate balance of creation. Her worship was widespread, with temples dedicated to her honor, where priestesses would perform rituals to invoke her blessings on the land and its people. Novah's legacy as Damkina endured, a testament to the enduring power of feminine energy and the mysteries of life.",
